WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.698
Your phone can find you on Earth to within
a few meters.

2
00:00:03.698 --> 00:00:06.636
Yet not one GPS satellite is tracking it.

3
00:00:06.636 --> 00:00:09.020
They only broadcast, like lighthouses.

4
00:00:10.705 --> 00:00:14.823
So how does a phone find itself from
signals that only travel one way?

5
00:00:14.823 --> 00:00:20.301
By measuring time, so precisely that one
billionth of a second is thirty

6
00:00:20.301 --> 00:00:21.625
centimeters of error.

7
00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:25.301
And the clocks involved are strange:

8
00:00:25.301 --> 00:00:31.109
without correction, a GPS satellite clock
would gain about thirty-eight microseconds

9
00:00:31.109 --> 00:00:33.720
a day compared with one on the ground.

10
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:39.050
That is far beyond the timing accuracy GPS
needs, and Einstein explains why.

11
00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:41.773
First, the geometry.

12
00:00:41.773 --> 00:00:44.917
Suppose you are exactly five kilometers
from a tower.

13
00:00:44.917 --> 00:00:48.278
You could be anywhere on a circle around
it.

14
00:00:48.278 --> 00:00:49.850
One distance is not enough.

15
00:00:51.545 --> 00:00:55.974
On a flat map, a second tower gives a
second circle, and the two cross in

16
00:00:55.974 --> 00:01:00.209
two places. A third circle leaves just one
spot where all three meet.

17
00:01:00.209 --> 00:01:02.123
Positioning from three

18
00:01:02.123 --> 00:01:04.345
distances: trilateration.

19
00:01:05.945 --> 00:01:09.794
The real world has three dimensions, so
each distance is a sphere.

20
00:01:09.794 --> 00:01:14.341
Three spheres can still meet at two
points, so a receiver needs more

21
00:01:14.341 --> 00:01:15.718
measurements than that.

22
00:01:15.718 --> 00:01:19.805
One extra measurement turns out to matter
for a surprising reason.

23
00:01:19.889 --> 00:01:24.866
All of this assumes the towers know where
they are.

24
00:01:24.866 --> 00:01:30.358
For GPS, monitor stations track every
satellite and work out its exact orbit and

25
00:01:30.358 --> 00:01:31.657
clock errors.

26
00:01:31.657 --> 00:01:36.891
Ground antennas then upload fresh data,
about every two hours under normal

27
00:01:36.891 --> 00:01:40.595
conditions, and the satellite rebroadcasts
it to users.

28
00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:47.102
The satellites orbit about twenty thousand
two hundred kilometers up, roughly three

29
00:01:47.102 --> 00:01:52.460
Earth radii above your head, moving about
fourteen thousand kilometers an hour.

30
00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:54.660
Each one circles the planet twice a day.

31
00:01:56.645 --> 00:02:00.978
There are at least twenty-four of them, in
six orbital planes tilted fifty-five

32
00:02:00.978 --> 00:02:02.264
degrees to the equator.

33
00:02:02.264 --> 00:02:07.860
That arrangement lets you see at least
four satellites from virtually any point

34
00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:12.458
on the planet. Each satellite broadcasts a
message:

35
00:02:12.458 --> 00:02:17.170
where it is, what time its clock says, how
healthy it is, and even a model of

36
00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:18.127
the ionosphere.

37
00:02:18.127 --> 00:02:20.195
Your phone only has to listen.

38
00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:26.805
Radio waves travel at the speed of light,
almost three hundred thousand kilometers

39
00:02:26.805 --> 00:02:29.938
a second. Distance is then speed times
travel time.

40
00:02:29.938 --> 00:02:33.530
Measure the time, and you know the
distance.

41
00:02:34.855 --> 00:02:39.591
From straight overhead, the signal takes
about seven hundredths of a second.

42
00:02:39.591 --> 00:02:42.940
Lower in the sky, up to about nine
hundredths.

43
00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:48.007
Short, but a clock that is off by just one
microsecond moves the answer by three

44
00:02:48.007 --> 00:02:48.935
hundred meters.

45
00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:54.977
The satellites carry atomic clocks that
the ground system keeps checking.

46
00:02:54.977 --> 00:03:00.115
Your receiver uses an inexpensive clock
that drifts, so it has to cope with its

47
00:03:00.115 --> 00:03:03.344
own error. Here is the trick.

48
00:03:03.344 --> 00:03:07.534
Suppose every distance comes out too long
by the same amount.

49
00:03:07.534 --> 00:03:11.479
On a flat map, the three circles no longer
meet at one point.

50
00:03:11.479 --> 00:03:16.620
Shrink them all equally until they do, and
that amount is the clock error.

51
00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:20.877
In three dimensions, that makes four
unknowns:

52
00:03:20.877 --> 00:03:24.825
east-west, north-south, height, and the
clock error.

53
00:03:24.825 --> 00:03:29.458
The receiver therefore uses at least four
satellites and solves for all of them

54
00:03:29.458 --> 00:03:34.150
at once. A distance with this clock error
baked in is called a pseudorange.

55
00:03:35.795 --> 00:03:40.645
As a bonus, the receiver now knows the
time, straight from the atomic clocks on

56
00:03:40.645 --> 00:03:41.896
the satellites.

57
00:03:41.896 --> 00:03:46.986
That timing synchronizes communications
systems and power grids, and lets

58
00:03:46.986 --> 00:03:50.284
financial transactions carry traceable
time tags.

59
00:03:50.284 --> 00:03:52.575
Hollywood even uses it in movie slates.

60
00:03:54.325 --> 00:03:58.457
Now those atomic clocks have a problem,
and it comes from Einstein.

61
00:03:58.457 --> 00:04:02.084
Special relativity says moving clocks tick
slower.

62
00:04:02.084 --> 00:04:07.788
At fourteen thousand kilometers an hour,
satellite clocks fall behind ground clocks

63
00:04:07.788 --> 00:04:09.785
by about seven microseconds a day.

64
00:04:11.445 --> 00:04:15.476
General relativity says clocks tick faster
where gravity is weaker.

65
00:04:15.476 --> 00:04:19.299
Up there, that adds about forty-five
microseconds a day.

66
00:04:19.299 --> 00:04:24.885
Combined with the effect of motion, an
uncorrected satellite clock would run

67
00:04:24.885 --> 00:04:28.845
about thirty-eight microseconds a day
faster than one on the ground.

68
00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.431
About thirty-eight microseconds a day
sounds tiny.

69
00:04:33.431 --> 00:04:38.763
But a ranging error of three meters takes
only ten nanoseconds of clock error.

70
00:04:38.763 --> 00:04:41.820
The offset is thousands of times too big
to ignore.

71
00:04:43.745 --> 00:04:44.720
Here is the twist.

72
00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:49.640
In a simplified model with identical
circular satellite orbits, ignoring

73
00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:54.409
relativity would make every measured
distance appear too short by the same

74
00:04:54.409 --> 00:04:58.660
amount, up to about eleven kilometers
between daily clock updates.

75
00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:03.629
That shared error could be absorbed into
the receiver's own clock bias, so the

76
00:05:03.629 --> 00:05:07.264
position estimate would survive in this
idealized case.

77
00:05:07.264 --> 00:05:08.309
GPS time would not:

78
00:05:08.309 --> 00:05:11.765
it could drift by thirty-eight
microseconds a day.

79
00:05:13.575 --> 00:05:18.589
Before launch, each satellite clock is
tuned to run slightly slow, by about four

80
00:05:18.589 --> 00:05:20.641
and a half parts in ten billion.

81
00:05:20.641 --> 00:05:24.035
Once in orbit, relativity cancels that
offset.

82
00:05:25.695 --> 00:05:30.233
Orbits are not perfect circles, so a
smaller relativistic error is left over,

83
00:05:30.233 --> 00:05:33.607
and it differs from satellite to
satellite.

84
00:05:33.607 --> 00:05:38.905
For a slightly oval orbit it can reach
about forty-five nanoseconds, roughly

85
00:05:38.905 --> 00:05:42.855
fifteen meters of range error, and
receivers correct for it.

86
00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:46.802
How does a receiver time a signal at all?

87
00:05:46.802 --> 00:05:48.888
Every satellite sends its own code:

88
00:05:48.888 --> 00:05:54.420
a pattern of one thousand twenty-three
pulses, repeating every millisecond.

89
00:05:56.095 --> 00:06:00.134
The receiver makes the same pattern and
slides it until the two match.

90
00:06:00.134 --> 00:06:04.642
That shift is how late the signal arrived,
by the receiver's own clock.

91
00:06:04.642 --> 00:06:07.615
That is why the result is called a
pseudorange.

92
00:06:09.305 --> 00:06:14.328
The pattern repeats every millisecond,
about three hundred kilometers of signal,

93
00:06:14.328 --> 00:06:18.296
so the code alone fixes the delay only
within one millisecond.

94
00:06:18.296 --> 00:06:20.745
The message supplies the rest:

95
00:06:20.745 --> 00:06:25.761
at fifty bits a second, each six-second
block carries a time-of-week word near its

96
00:06:25.761 --> 00:06:29.985
start, so the receiver can work out which
millisecond it is hearing.

97
00:06:31.675 --> 00:06:36.159
Between the satellite and your phone, the
signal crosses the ionosphere and the

98
00:06:36.159 --> 00:06:38.625
troposphere, and both add delay.

99
00:06:38.625 --> 00:06:43.809
Dry air alone adds about two point three
meters straight overhead, and about ten

100
00:06:43.809 --> 00:06:46.419
meters for satellites low on the horizon.

101
00:06:46.419 --> 00:06:51.379
Water vapor adds only tens of centimeters,
but it changes quickly and is hard

102
00:06:51.379 --> 00:06:57.716
to model. The ionosphere changes with the
time of day and with solar activity.

103
00:06:57.716 --> 00:07:02.077
It delays lower frequencies more, in
proportion to one over the

104
00:07:02.077 --> 00:07:03.580
frequency squared.

105
00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:09.024
A receiver listening on two frequencies
can compare the delays and correct for it.

106
00:07:09.024 --> 00:07:13.465
With the newer civil signal L2C, civilian
receivers can do that.

107
00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:17.913
In cities, signals also bounce off
buildings.

108
00:07:17.913 --> 00:07:23.547
Engineers call it multipath, and it makes
a signal look as if it traveled farther.

109
00:07:23.547 --> 00:07:28.650
Where buildings, bridges or trees block
the signal, the blue dot gets worse.

110
00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:33.526
Put it all together, and a pseudorange is
simply a sum:

111
00:07:33.526 --> 00:07:38.859
the true distance, plus the clock errors,
plus the ionosphere and troposphere

112
00:07:38.859 --> 00:07:41.132
delays, plus multipath and noise.

113
00:07:41.132 --> 00:07:44.040
The receiver's whole job is to untangle
that sum.

114
00:07:45.895 --> 00:07:47.669
Accuracy comes in layers. The U.S.

115
00:07:47.669 --> 00:07:52.103
government commits to a daily global
average signal-in-space ranging error of

116
00:07:52.103 --> 00:07:56.638
at most two meters, with ninety-five
percent probability, across

117
00:07:56.638 --> 00:07:58.138
healthy satellites.

118
00:07:58.138 --> 00:08:02.723
On one day in 2021, that average was at
most about sixty-four centimeters.

119
00:08:02.723 --> 00:08:06.431
But that does not describe your phone's
accuracy.

120
00:08:06.431 --> 00:08:11.377
Your position also depends on satellite
geometry, the atmosphere and nearby

121
00:08:11.377 --> 00:08:16.015
buildings, so phones typically land within
about five meters under open sky.

122
00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:19.465
It used to be worse by design.

123
00:08:19.465 --> 00:08:24.593
Until May 2000, the United States
deliberately degraded the public GPS

124
00:08:24.593 --> 00:08:27.049
signal for national security reasons.

125
00:08:27.049 --> 00:08:32.300
Then it turned that feature off, and says
it has no intent to use it again.

126
00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:35.433
Engineers push further.

127
00:08:35.433 --> 00:08:38.922
Augmentation systems add corrections;

128
00:08:38.922 --> 00:08:43.320
WAAS, run by the FAA, helps aircraft
navigate across North America.

129
00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:50.605
Newer civil signals, called L2C, L5 and
L1C, are being added next to the original

130
00:08:50.605 --> 00:08:53.984
one, but receivers must be upgraded to use
them.

131
00:08:53.984 --> 00:08:59.770
Other nations fly their own
constellations, like Galileo in Europe.

132
00:09:01.295 --> 00:09:05.523
When the blue dot lands on the right
street, here is what just happened.

133
00:09:05.523 --> 00:09:09.553
Signals that left the satellites a
fraction of a second ago were timed to

134
00:09:09.553 --> 00:09:14.002
within billionths of a second, then
corrected for the atmosphere and

135
00:09:14.002 --> 00:09:14.875
for relativity.

136
00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:18.399
And it is not only for phones.

137
00:09:18.399 --> 00:09:24.049
Spacecraft use GPS as a compass, and
sometimes even as a clock, replacing

138
00:09:24.049 --> 00:09:26.360
expensive atomic clocks on board.

139
00:09:27.945 --> 00:09:30.326
Relativity is not only for satellites.

140
00:09:30.326 --> 00:09:35.813
NIST physicists once ran two clocks just a
foot apart in height, and they ticked at

141
00:09:35.813 --> 00:09:37.175
different rates.

142
00:09:37.175 --> 00:09:42.723
Gravity changes how time passes, and every
time that blue dot appears, GPS has

143
00:09:42.723 --> 00:09:43.765
accounted for it.

144
00:09:45.275 --> 00:09:47.239
Every claim in this video is sourced.

145
00:09:47.239 --> 00:09:48.875
The list is in the description.
